/* A DISJUNCT chosen from the remembered copy that the disjunctively
   well-founded (dwf) reduction adds, where the two ranking pieces that make
   the cheat work are exactly the ones the honest parts of the problem ask for.

   Verdict: INVALID.  Every y <> 0 has exactly one successor n(y) that R y
   demands (1 -> 3 -> 2 -> 0, y >= 4 -> y - 1, y <= -1 -> y + 1), and every
   orbit of n reaches 0, where y <> 0 fails; so R is false everywhere.  Then
   the body of P maps the empty predicate to the empty predicate, so the least
   fixpoint P is false everywhere and P 0 is false.

   The reduction turns P into the greatest-fixpoint predicate P'(c, x), c the
   remembered copy, and each call P(z) into
   P'(z, z) /\ P'(c, z) /\ WF_P(c, z).  Take WF_P as the union of
     r (a, b) := a > b /\ a >= 0     (rank a,  asked by the chains x >= 2 downwards)
     r'(a, b) := a < b /\ a <= 0     (rank -a, asked by the chains x <= -1 upwards)
   each a one-piece bounded linear ranking, so the union is disjunctively
   well-founded.  Then
     I(c, x) := (x >= 2 => c >= x) /\ (x <= -1 => c <= x)
   is a post-fixpoint of the reduced equation that contains every (x, x): in
   the region {0, 1} take P 0 when c <> 0 and P 1 when c = 0, a choice that
   reads the copy c.  So the reduced query forall x. P'(x, x) holds, and a
   check that leaves the disjunct to the greatest fixpoint is satisfiable.  A
   choice that reads only x makes a real play cycle inside {0, 1}, and the
   record-keeping branch then needs (v, v) in WF_P for a repeated value v.

   Compared with mustrat_dwfparam_disjunct_invalid.hes: there every play of
   the refuter's check starts at 0, so each ranking piece a one-piece template
   can return contains (0, 1) and none contains (1, 0), and the cheat is
   unreachable.  Here the query is universal, so plays can start outside the
   cycle, and the two pieces come from different plays.  R's chain
   1 -> 3 -> 2 -> 0 is ordered by no linear rank, which is meant to keep the
   refuter from winning before the prover has both pieces.

   A correct solver answers invalid.  An answer of valid is the unsound
   disjunct choice realised. */
forall (x: int). P x
s.t.
P (x: int): bool =mu (x >= 2 /\ P (x - 1)) \/ (x <= -1 /\ P (x + 1)) \/ (0 <= x /\ x <= 1 /\ (P 0 \/ P 1)) \/ R x;
R (y: int): bool =nu y != 0 /\ (y = 1 => R 3) /\ (y = 3 => R 2) /\ (y = 2 => R 0) /\ (y >= 4 => R (y - 1)) /\ (y <= -1 => R (y + 1));
